    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "The relation you are auditing",
      "prov:value": "The layer that ships such a content type holds that\ncomparison and names this skill from its side (for a paper folio, the science\nlayer; bean `syiq`).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean syiq"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`interprets` is the same relation under a second field name",
      "prov:value": "`uses[]` is the curated list. **`interprets` states the same reader-facing fact**\nfor a remark or example *about one specific block*, and the content graph counts\n**both** — since 2026-08-15, bean `i8ad`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean i8ad",
        "2026-08-15"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-08-15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`interprets` is the same relation under a second field name",
      "prov:value": "- **`detangler-no-forward-ref` builds its own `uses`-only adjacency** and does\n  not consume the shared graph, so it is unaffected by `interprets` — and the\n  forward-pointing `interprets` edges are outside what it counts. Ten were, when\n  this was measured.\n- **The editorial graph is no longer acyclic.** Genuine editorial cycles are\n  *revealed* by counting both fields, not introduced by it. Do not \"fix\" one\n  without reading whether it is real."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`interprets` is the same relation under a second field name",
      "prov:value": "**Do not quote a count from prose — run the check against the corpus in front of\nyou.** The forward-reference count was 1 when `i8ad` was measured and 4 a few\nhours later on merged content, none of it caused by the change. A number in a\ndocument is a claim; only a run is evidence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Forward references and `foreshadows[]`",
      "prov:value": "**`foreshadows[]` is independent of `uses[]`** (owner ruling, 2026-08-10;\nthe old `foreshadows-subset-of-uses` rule is gone). That independence is\nthe whole point, and it splits the field into two cases you must keep\napart:\n\n| case | in `uses[]`? | who records it | cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Pure forward pointer** — the prose names something coming later and does not depend on it | no | **derived** from the `.md`, not authored | zero by construction: it never enters the dependency graph |\n| **Deferred prerequisite** — a real dependency the paper states later on purpose | yes | **declared** by the author, and only the author | exempt from ordering *cost*; still counted for cycles, cones and depth |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-08-10",
        "owner"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-08-10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Forward references and `foreshadows[]`",
      "prov:value": "The earlier subset rule required every foreshadow to sit in `uses[]`,\nwhich made the field an annotation on an edge and nothing more. It could\nnot express the case it was most wanted for — a chapter overview naming\nresults it previews but does not depend on — because recording such a\npointer meant first asserting a dependency that is not real."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Forward references and `foreshadows[]`",
      "prov:value": "Note what the derivation does **not** do: it never reads deferral\n*language*. It matches links and labels. An attempt to detect phrasing\ninstead classified \"**Below** $n^*$\" — a numeric comparison — as a\ndeferral, which is why the mechanical half stays mechanical."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Declaring the second case",
      "prov:value": "The bar is a first-person authorial hand-off that says the treatment\nhappens *elsewhere*: \"we establish this in X\", \"we work this out\nseparately\", \"the derivation is recorded in X\". In a review of 274\nforward edges, every declaration that survived adversarial re-review had\nthat shape, and **17 of 23** first-pass declarations did not and were\nwithdrawn. Read that number in context: it was measured under the subset\nrule, when *every* forward pointer had to be declared. Most of the\nwithdrawn ones were pure pointers, which now derive themselves."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Declaring the second case",
      "prov:value": "Two rules police the field now that the subset requirement is gone:\n`foreshadows-resolve` (every target must exist — the referential\nintegrity `uses[]` validation used to provide incidentally) and\n`foreshadows-not-self`. The `foreshadows-point-forward` criterion catches\na target the reader has already passed, which is either a prerequisite\nfiled in the wrong field or an inert back-reference."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-10",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A block's `uses[]` may be carrying the whole family",
      "prov:value": "- **Proof children.** A proof cites what the statement assumes. In one case the\n  parent's prose genuinely never named the target, its **proof child cited the\n  target by label** for the exact identity the parent's boxed formula is built\n  from — and that proof declared no `uses[]` of its own. The parent's entry was\n  the **sole record** of the dependency, so deleting it as \"unsupported by the\n  prose\" would have erased the edge entirely."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-11",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Sibling parity is evidence only until a bulk edit touched the family",
      "prov:value": "Two independent readers used it to judge a five-member family of millennium-\nproblem bounds *deliberately* empty — each names the same six blocks in prose\nand each declares nothing. The uniformity was real. It was also **uniform\ndamage**: every member lost 8-10 edges to a single \"transitive-prune\" commit\nwhose rule (*if A→B and B→C, drop A→C*) fired where B→C was being dropped in the\nsame pass. A bulk edit produces parity **by construction**, so parity cannot\ndistinguish a shared convention from a shared wound."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-12",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "One reading is not the standard",
      "prov:value": "- A pass over 274 forward edges had its **destructive verdicts re-reviewed**:\n  3 of 35 deletions and **17 of 23** foreshadow declarations were overturned."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/authoring/authoring-core/uses-editorial-review.md#provenance-13",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "One reading is not the standard",
      "prov:value": "**A retarget carries both burdens.** It asserts this edge is wrong *and* that\none is right, so it needs evidence for each half separately. In the slice above\none retarget's second half was true — the proposed target really was leaned on\nand really was missing — while its first half was false. Split, it was not a\nswap but an **addition**, and both edges survived."
    }
  ]
}
